                "sender": "w-nws.webmaster@noaa.gov",
                "senderName": "NWS Brownsville TX",
                "headline": "Flood Watch issued November 7 at 11:34AM CST until November 9 at 9:00AM CST by NWS Brownsville TX",
                "description": "Multiple rounds of locally heavy rainfall are expected between\nSaturday night and early Monday morning across the Rio Grande Plains\nand Brush Country.  The first round is most likely between midnight\nand mid-morning Sunday, when 1 to 2 inches with locally 3 or more\ninches of rain may fall. The second round is expected Sunday night\ninto early Monday morning, when an additional 1 to 3 inches with\nlocally 4 inches or more may fall.\n\n* WHAT...Flooding caused by excessive rainfall is possible.\n\n* WHERE...A portion of Deep South Texas, including the following\ncounties, Brooks, Jim Hogg and Zapata.\n\n* WHEN...From 6 PM CST this evening through Monday morning.\n\n* IMPACTS...Excessive runoff may result in flooding of arroyos and\nstreams, and other low-lying and flood-prone locations. Some\narroyos may rise out of their banks near where heaviest rains fall.\nFlooding may occur in poor drainage and urban areas. Storm drains\nand ditches may become clogged with debris. Highest concern\nincludes locations in Zapata, such as Medina Addition, as well as\nmore populated areas of Hebbronville and Falfurrias, including\nalong state highways 16, 359, and 285.\n\n* ADDITIONAL DETAILS...\n- Potential for heavy rainfall, including two windows where\nrainfall rates could reach 2 inches per hour for two hours both\nlate tonight and early Sunday morning, and again Sunday night\nthrough mid morning Monday.\n- Concern is highest across populated areas, including the cities\nof Zapata, Hebbronville, and Falfurrias.  Flood prone areas along\nor near arroyos in Jim Hogg and Zapata are at most risk, but high\nwater could also flood open rangeland.\n- http://www.weather.gov/safety/flood",
                "instruction": "You should monitor later forecasts and be alert for possible Flood\nWarnings. Those living in areas prone to flooding should be prepared\nto take action should flooding develop.",
